# NorthNarrow COMBAT-state iptables ruleset (Tappa 7 task 7 / Tappa 8).
#
# Loaded by NetworkIsolator::engage() via `iptables-restore --noflush`.
# ADDITIVE (BUG-041): COMBAT ADDS an isolation layer, it does NOT become
# the firewall. The `:NORTHNARROW_COMBAT` declaration atomically rebuilds
# OUR chain (re-reading the carve-out); the `-I … 1` jumps insert it on
# TOP of the operator's INPUT/OUTPUT/FORWARD rules; `--noflush` leaves
# every operator chain/rule untouched. During COMBAT our chain is
# TERMINAL — it decides every packet (ACCEPT lo + carve-out, DROP the
# rest), so the operator's rules sit beneath it, inert but PRESERVED, and
# are uncovered intact when teardown removes our jumps + chain.
#
# Why our chain is terminal (lo = ACCEPT, not RETURN): under the additive
# model a RETURN would fall through to the operator's rules, making our
# loopback guarantee depend on their config. ACCEPT keeps it ours.
#
# Beta Step 4b — OPT-IN management carve-out. engage() replaces the
# `# >>> NORTHNARROW_MGMT_CARVEOUT <<<` marker with `-j ACCEPT` rules for
# each IPv4 CIDR in /etc/northnarrow/combat-allow.cidrs (default-empty =
# full isolation). Lets a remote operator keep an SSH path from a
# declared management network during COMBAT. Recovery otherwise requires
# an Ed25519-signed admin unlock (agent/src/anti_tamper/admin_auth.rs;
# docs/operator/COMBAT_RECOVERY.md).
#
# Keep the marker immediately before the catch-all DROP so carve-out
# ACCEPTs are evaluated ahead of it.

*filter
:NORTHNARROW_COMBAT - [0:0]
-A NORTHNARROW_COMBAT -i lo -j ACCEPT
-A NORTHNARROW_COMBAT -o lo -j ACCEPT
# >>> NORTHNARROW_MGMT_CARVEOUT <<<
-A NORTHNARROW_COMBAT -j DROP
-I INPUT 1 -j NORTHNARROW_COMBAT
-I OUTPUT 1 -j NORTHNARROW_COMBAT
-I FORWARD 1 -j NORTHNARROW_COMBAT
COMMIT
